Open Snorra Edda — edda-data
Licensing summary

1. Source texts

   The medieval works themselves (Snorri's Edda, the Eddic poems) are in the public domain. The editions
   used here have different statuses:

   - Old Norse: Edda Snorra Sturlusonar (1954) and Eddukvæði (1949), normalised editions by Guðni Jónsson
     (Íslendingasagnaútgáfan), as published by heimskringla.no. EDITION RIGHTS NOT YET CONFIRMED. Guðni
     Jónsson died in 1974; if a normalised edition is protected as the editor's own work, protection could
     run to the end of 2044. heimskringla.no publishes the texts freely and asks to be credited; please do.
     Permission is being requested from heimskringla.no. If it is not given, the Old Norse column will be
     re-based on a source whose status is clear (a 19th-century edition, or a new normalisation made from
     the manuscript transcriptions), keeping row ids.
   - Modern Icelandic: Gylfaginning, Netútgáfan, June 1997 (https://netutgafan.snerpa.is/snorri/gylf.htm).
     Published freely online; its terms of use are NOT YET CONFIRMED.
   - English: Arthur Gilchrist Brodeur, The Prose Edda (American-Scandinavian Foundation, 1916), via the
     sacred-texts.com transcription. Public domain.
   - English: Henry Adams Bellows, The Poetic Edda (American-Scandinavian Foundation, 1923), via the
     Wikisource transcription. Public domain (Bellows died in 1939).

   No new rights are claimed over these texts or over the automatic alignment and modernisation derived
   from them (aligned/, auto/). Until the two open points above are settled, do not describe the whole
   dataset as public domain.

2. Contributions (CC BY 4.0)

   Everything added by contributors — overlay records in overlays/, image records and captions in images/,
   documentation — is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International licence (CC BY 4.0).

   You are free to share and adapt this material for any purpose, including commercially, provided you give
   appropriate credit (name the project and the contributors listed in the records), link to the licence
   and indicate if changes were made.

   Full legal code: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode
   Human-readable summary: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

   By contributing you agree that your contribution is licensed CC BY 4.0. The project is considering a
   change to CC0 1.0 for contributions; contributors will be asked before any such change applies to their
   work.

